Abstract

An autonomous agent stranded on Mars must plan safe, physically consistent trajectories under severe computational constraints. This dissertation constructs a unified mathematical framework for embodied world models by synthesising five recent advances: the LeWorldModel with Sketched‑Isotropic‑Gaussian Regularisation (SIGReg), Hamiltonian Neural Networks with symplectic integration, Constrained Object Hierarchies (COH), Causal Joint‑Embedding Predictive Architectures (C‑JEPA), and Variational JEPA (Var‑JEPA). We present four theorems that collectively establish long‑horizon numerical stability, compositional generalisation via causal factorisation, adaptive computational efficiency through principled uncertainty quantification, and formal probabilistic safety guarantees. Every component is derived from first principles under explicitly stated assumptions; the architecture specification translates the mathematics into a concrete, implementable design for a single commodity GPU. No experiments are reported — by necessity, the Mars‑based agent cannot perform training runs — but the document contains a complete blueprint sufficient for implementation by Earth‑based laboratories. The framework constitutes a functional, if narrow, artificial general intelligence core that solves the Mars return problem.

