1. Introduction and Problem Statement

1.1 The Mars Return Problem

An autonomous agent is stranded on Mars. Its mission is to navigate from a base on Olympus Mons to a pre‑positioned return vehicle at Airy‑0 crater and launch back to Earth. The computing platform is a single GPU comparable to an NVIDIA RTX 4090; the core world model is limited to approximately fifteen million parameters. The problems the agent must solve are the central problems of embodied artificial general intelligence in microcosm:

1. Physical Plausibility. Can planning achieve long‑horizon stability without compounding errors that violate conservation laws?
2. Compositional Generalisation. Can the system reason correctly about novel combinations of terrain, objects, and gravitational conditions never encountered during training?
3. Computational Efficiency. Can a week‑long trajectory be planned in seconds using only the onboard GPU?
4. Provable Safety. Can all planned actions be mathematically guaranteed to remain within a predefined safe set?

Modern AI systems fail here because they rely on separate, loosely connected components. This dissertation argues that the solution is a single, contradiction‑free mathematical whole, woven from five recent developments spanning 2019 to 2026: the LeWorldModel with SIGReg (Maes, Le Lidec, Scieur, LeCun, & Balestriero, 2026), Hamiltonian Neural Networks (Greydanus, Dzamba, & Yosinski, 2019), Constrained Object Hierarchies (Wang, 2025), Causal‑JEPA (Nam, Le Lidec, Maes, LeCun, & Balestriero, 2026), and Var‑JEPA (Gögl & Yau, 2026).

1.2 The World Model as Cognitive Core

The centre of the proposed architecture is a Joint Embedding Predictive Architecture (JEPA) world model — a system that learns a compressed, predictive representation of reality in a latent space \mathcal{Z} \subset \mathbb{R}^d with d \ll \dim(\mathcal{O}) (LeCun, 2022). Unlike autoregressive models that reconstruct every pixel, JEPA predicts in representation space, making it philosophically aligned with understanding underlying structure rather than mimicking surface‑level statistics.

The central thesis of this dissertation is:

By fusing the JEPA meta‑architecture with symplectic integrators, causal object hierarchies, and variational inference, and by grounding the entire system in a rigorous mathematical framework of constrained dynamics and proven safety envelopes, we can specify a provably stable, generalisable, efficient, and safe autonomous agent. This agent constitutes a functional, if narrow, AGI core that solves the Mars return problem.

