4. Theorem 1: Symplectic Stochastic Embedding

The first gap we address is the lack of physical structure in the learned latent transition f_\theta. An unconstrained neural network predictor may generate states that violate conservation laws, leading to compounding errors over long horizons. We resolve this by embedding a symplectic integrator into the predictive architecture.

4.1 Preliminaries on Hamiltonian Systems

We briefly recall the essential concepts from symplectic geometry (Arnold, 1989; Hairer, Lubich, & Wanner, 2006). A symplectic manifold (\mathcal{M}, \omega) is a smooth, even‑dimensional manifold \mathcal{M} equipped with a closed, non‑degenerate 2‑form \omega. In local Darboux coordinates x = (q_1, \ldots, q_n, p_1, \ldots, p_n), the symplectic form takes the canonical expression \omega = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \mathrm{d}q_i \wedge \mathrm{d}p_i. Given a smooth Hamiltonian function H: \mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R}, the associated Hamiltonian vector field X_H is defined by \iota_{X_H} \omega = -\mathrm{d}H. The flow \phi_t^{H} of X_H preserves the symplectic form and conserves the Hamiltonian: H \circ \phi_t^{H} = H.

A numerical one‑step method \Psi_{\Delta t}: \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{M} is called symplectic if \Psi_{\Delta t}^* \omega = \omega. Symplectic integrators benefit from backward error analysis: the numerical trajectory is exponentially close, over exponentially long times, to the exact trajectory of a slightly perturbed Hamiltonian system. For an integrator of order p, the one‑step error satisfies \|\Psi_{\Delta t}(x) - \phi_{\Delta t}^{H}(x)\| \leq C (\Delta t)^{p+1} uniformly on compact subsets of \mathcal{M}.

4.2 Statement of the Theorem

Theorem 4.1 (Symplectic Stochastic Embedding). Let the true physical system evolve under a Hamiltonian H_{\text{true}} on a symplectic manifold (\mathcal{M}, \omega). Let e : \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{Z} be a SIGReg‑trained encoder such that P_{\text{emp}} is \varepsilon-close to \mathcal{N}(0, I_d) in W_1 distance (Lemma 3.1). Assume there exists a smooth approximate decoder d : \mathcal{Z} \to \mathcal{M} with reconstruction error \|d(e(x)) - x\|_{\mathcal{M}} \leq \delta for all x in the safe operating region \mathcal{S} \subset \mathcal{M}. Then there exists a latent Hamiltonian H_{\mathcal{Z}} = H_{\text{true}} \circ d and a symplectic integrator \Psi_{\Delta t} of order p such that

\| e \circ \phi_{\Delta t}^{H_{\text{true}}} - \Psi_{\Delta t} \circ e \|_{L^2(\mathcal{S})} \leq C_1 \delta + C_2 (\Delta t)^{p+1},

where C_1 and C_2 are constants depending on the Lipschitz constants of H_{\text{true}} and the Jacobian of the decoder.

4.3 Proof

The proof proceeds in three steps.

Step 1: Latent Symplectic Structure. Define the pullback 2‑form \omega_{\mathcal{Z}} = e^* \omega on \mathcal{Z}. Since e is smooth, \omega_{\mathcal{Z}} is closed. Non‑degeneracy follows from the approximate invertibility provided by the decoder: for any non‑zero tangent vector v \in T_z \mathcal{Z} with z = e(x), the vector De_x^{-1}(v) (well‑defined up to the reconstruction error via the pseudo‑inverse of the Jacobian) is non‑zero, and \omega_{\mathcal{Z}}(v, \cdot) cannot vanish identically.

By the quantitative Darboux theorem (Moser, 1965; cf. McDuff & Salamon, 2017), any closed, sufficiently non‑degenerate 2‑form that is C^0-close to the canonical form \omega_{\text{can}} can be mapped to \omega_{\text{can}} by a diffeomorphism whose distance from the identity is bounded linearly in the perturbation. The reconstruction bound \|d \circ e - \text{id}\| \leq \delta implies that \|\omega_{\mathcal{Z}} - \omega_{\text{can}}\|_{\infty} \leq C' \delta. Hence there exists a symplectomorphism \Phi : \mathcal{Z} \to \mathcal{Z} with \Phi^* \omega_{\text{can}} = \omega_{\mathcal{Z}} and \|\Phi - \text{id}\| \leq C'' \delta.

Step 2: Latent Hamiltonian Construction. Define the latent Hamiltonian as

H_{\mathcal{Z}}(z) = H_{\text{true}}(d(z)).

The Hamiltonian vector field of H_{\mathcal{Z}} with respect to \omega_{\mathcal{Z}} is, by construction, conjugate to the true vector field X_{H_{\text{true}}} via the encoder–decoder pair: up to terms of order \delta, the latent flow pushed through the encoder coincides with the true flow.

Step 3: Symplectic Integration Error. Let \tilde{\Psi}_{\Delta t} be a symplectic integrator of order p for the latent Hamiltonian system (H_{\mathcal{Z}}, \omega_{\mathcal{Z}}). By the standard convergence theory of symplectic integrators (Hairer, Lubich, & Wanner, 2006, Theorem VIII.3.1),

\| \tilde{\Psi}_{\Delta t}(z) - \phi_{\Delta t}^{H_{\mathcal{Z}}}(z) \| \leq C_2 (\Delta t)^{p+1}

for all z in a compact subset of \mathcal{Z}. The total error is the sum of the reconstruction error and the integration error, yielding the stated bound. ∎

4.4 Architectural Specification

The deterministic predictor f_\theta(z, a) is replaced by a Symplectic Hamiltonian Block (SHB). The learnable symplectic embedding S_\theta : \mathcal{Z} \to \mathbb{R}^{d/2} \times \mathbb{R}^{d/2} maps the latent state into canonical coordinates (q, p). To guarantee that S_\theta is symplectic by construction, we parameterise it as the exponential of a Hamiltonian matrix: S_\theta = \exp(\Sigma) with \Sigma^{\top} J + J \Sigma = 0, where J = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & I_{d/2} \\ -I_{d/2} & 0 \end{pmatrix}. This ensures that S_\theta^* \omega_{\text{can}} = \omega_{\mathcal{Z}} and that the inverse map S_\theta^{-1} = -J S_\theta^{\top} J is always available.

The total training objective for the physics‑biased world model is

\mathcal{L}_{\text{PhyWM}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{pred}} + \lambda_1 \mathcal{L}_{\text{SIGReg}} + \lambda_2 \| H_\theta(q_{t+1}, p_{t+1}) - H_\theta(q_t, p_t) \|_2^2,

where the third term penalises discrete Hamiltonian drift across one leapfrog step, implementing energy conservation in the discrete setting.

