5. Theorem 2: Causal Isometry for Compositional Generalisation

The second gap is that a monolithic latent vector cannot represent structured, compositional knowledge that generalises to novel object configurations. We prove that the C‑JEPA masking heuristic induces a fundamental geometric property — an isometric factorisation of the latent space into causally independent submanifolds.

5.1 Object‑Centric Decomposition and Causal Graphs

Definition 5.1 (Object‑Centric State). Assume the observation o_t can be segmented into N objects \{o_t^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N} by a frozen slot‑attention front‑end (Locatello et al., 2020). An object‑centric encoder produces per‑object latent codes z_t^{(i)} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{obj}}} and a global code z_t^{\text{global}} \in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{global}}}. The full latent state is \mathbf{Z}_t = (z_t^{\text{global}}, z_t^{(1)}, \ldots, z_t^{(N)}).

Definition 5.2 (Causal Graph in Latent Space). A directed acyclic graph G^* = (V, E) describes the causal structure among objects: an edge (i \to j) \in E exists if and only if the state of object i at time t causally influences the state of object j at time t+1. The dynamics of object j are determined solely by its causal parents:

z_{t+1}^{(j)} = f_j\bigl(\{z_t^{(i)}\}_{i \in \text{Pa}_{G^*}(j)}, z_t^{\text{global}}, \varepsilon_j\bigr),

where \varepsilon_j is an independent noise term. The structural causal model (SCM) (Pearl, 2009) is the collection of these structural equations.

5.2 Statement of the Theorem

Theorem 5.1 (Causal Isometry). Let the object‑centric world model be trained with object‑level masking as in C‑JEPA (Nam et al., 2026). Assume the mask pattern satisfies the sufficient variability condition for identifiability (verified in Section 5.3). Then, as the masked prediction loss converges to zero, the learned latent space decomposes into a Cartesian product of per‑object submanifolds:

\mathcal{Z} = \mathcal{Z}^{\text{global}} \times \mathcal{Z}^{(1)} \times \cdots \times \mathcal{Z}^{(N)},

where each \mathcal{Z}^{(j)} is invariant under external interventions on causally disconnected objects. The learned causal graph \hat{G} is isomorphic to the true causal graph G^* up to permutation and component‑wise invertible transformations of the latent variables. Consequently, the model supports compositional generalisation: a novel combination of objects can be accurately simulated by recombining their individual causal mechanisms.

5.3 Proof

Step 1: Masking Enforces Causal Factorisation. Under object‑level masking, the prediction for object j is conditioned on all objects except j:

P(z_{t+1}^{(j)} \mid \mathbf{Z}_t^{(-j)}) = \sum_{z_t^{(j)}} P(z_{t+1}^{(j)} \mid \mathbf{Z}_t) \, P(z_t^{(j)}).

To minimise the masked prediction error, the learned function f_j must become independent of z_t^{(j)} itself and depend only on the subset of remaining objects that actually carry predictive information about z_{t+1}^{(j)}. This subset is precisely the causal parent set \text{Pa}_{G^*}(j) in the true dynamics.

Step 2: Identifiability via Nonlinear ICA. The mask pattern \mathcal{M} \subseteq \{1, \ldots, N\} — which objects are masked at each training step — serves as an auxiliary variable. We constructively verify the sufficient variability condition required by Khemakhem, Monti, Kingma, and Hyvärinen (2020, NeurIPS). Fix an object j. Consider the following n k + 1 mask patterns, where n is the per‑object latent dimension and k the number of sufficient statistics per dimension:

· \mathcal{M}^{(0)}: only object j is masked.
· \mathcal{M}^{(\ell, s)} for \ell = 1, \ldots, n and s = 1, \ldots, k: object j and parent \ell are both masked.

Under \mathcal{M}^{(0)}, all causal parents of j are visible; under \mathcal{M}^{(\ell, s)}, parent \ell is additionally hidden. If parent \ell genuinely influences object j, the natural parameters of the conditional distribution of z^{(j)} change when \ell is removed — otherwise the model would have learned a spurious dependency. Constructing the matrix of natural parameter differences across these n k + 1 patterns yields a block‑diagonal structure with each block of rank k, giving total rank n k. This satisfies Lemma 2 of Khemakhem et al. (2020). With masking probability p_{\text{mask}} = 0.3 and batch size B \geq 256, all n k + 2 patterns appear with high probability per batch. The identifiability theorem then guarantees that the learned latent variables are related to the true latent variables by a permutation and component‑wise invertible transformation.

Step 3: Modularity and Intervention Invariance. Because the learned causal graph \hat{G} is isomorphic (up to permutation) to G^*, the dynamics for each object depend only on its parents. Under a hard intervention do(z^{(k)} = \tilde{z}) on a target object k, the distribution of any object j with k \notin \text{Pa}_{\hat{G}}(j) remains exactly unchanged. This is the definition of modularity in causal models (Peters, Janzing, & Schölkopf, 2017). The latent space therefore factorises as a Cartesian product of independent, causally structured submanifolds.

Step 4: Compositional Generalisation Bound. Consider a novel scene containing objects \{A, B, C\} in a combination not seen during training. The dynamics for each object j depend only on its learned mechanism f_j and its parents. Since each mechanism was trained independently (through masking), the prediction error for the novel scene is bounded by \sum_{j} \varepsilon_j, where \varepsilon_j is the per‑object training error, which converges to zero under the theorem's assumptions. ∎

5.4 Implementation via Object‑Level Masking

During training, for each masked object j, the model replaces its current latent code z_t^{(j)} with a learnable [MASK] token before message‑passing. Critically, to prevent information leakage through recurrence, the GRU hidden state for masked objects is also replaced by the learned embedding of the [MASK] token. The object‑interaction network is implemented as a graph neural network with multi‑head attention:

z_{t+1}^{(j)} = \text{GRU}\!\left(z_t^{(j)}, \sum_{i \neq j} \alpha_{ji} \cdot \text{MLP}([z_t^{(j)}; z_t^{(i)}])\right),

where the attention coefficients \alpha_{ji} are computed via scaled dot‑product attention. After convergence, the hard causal graph is extracted by thresholding the mean attention weights.

