7. Theorem 4: COH‑Bounded Safety (The Master Theorem)

The final gap is a guarantee that all planned actions remain provably safe. We prove a theorem that binds all previous components — symplectic stability, causal factorisation, and uncertainty‑gated computation — into a single, formally verifiable safety envelope within the COH framework.

7.1 Formalising Safety

Definition 7.1 (COH Safety Specification). Let the rover be a COH object with K hard constraint daemons \{D_i\}_{i=1}^{K}, each monitoring a safety predicate h_i : \mathcal{O} \to \mathbb{R} such that h_i(o) \geq 0 iff the constraint is satisfied. The true safe set is

\mathcal{S} = \bigl\{ o \in \mathcal{O} : \min_{i} h_i(o) \geq 0 \bigr\}.

Definition 7.2 (Latent Safety Abstraction). A family of continuous functions \hat{h}_i : \mathcal{Z} \to \mathbb{R} is a sound latent safety abstraction if \hat{h}_i(e(o)) \geq 0 \Rightarrow h_i(o) \geq 0 for all o \in \mathcal{S}.

7.2 Statement of the Master Theorem

Theorem 7.1 (COH‑Bounded Safety). Consider an agent whose perception and planning satisfy Theorems 4.1, 5.1, and 6.1. Then there exist latent barrier functions B_i : \mathcal{Z}^{(i)} \to \mathbb{R} for each COH daemon, defined on the causally factorised object submanifolds, and a safety filter such that for any action a_{\text{raw}} proposed by the planner, the filter outputs a_{\text{safe}} satisfying, for all i,

\nabla B_i(z)^{\top} f_\theta(z, a_{\text{safe}}) \geq -\alpha_i B_i(z) + \kappa \sqrt{U(z)},

where \alpha_i > 0 are class‑\mathcal{K} function parameters, \kappa > 0 is a margin scaling constant, and U(z) is the epistemic uncertainty from the Var‑JEPA module. Moreover, the probability of a safety violation over a horizon of N steps is bounded by

P(\text{unsafe}) \leq N \exp\!\left(-\frac{M_{\text{eff}} \, \varepsilon_{\text{conj}}^2}{\kappa^2 \tau}\right) + \mathcal{O}\!\bigl(N(\delta + \Delta t^{p+1})\bigr),

where \varepsilon_{\text{conj}} = C_1 \delta + C_2 (\Delta t)^{p+1} is the conjugacy error from Theorem 4.1, and M_{\text{eff}} = \rho M_{\text{base}} + (1 - \rho) M_{\text{high}}.

7.3 Proof

Step 1: Safety Factorisation via Causal Isometry. Theorem 5.1 guarantees that the latent space factorises into per‑object submanifolds. Each COH daemon D_i (e.g., rollover, battery depletion, collision) depends only on a small subset of objects — the rover chassis for tilt constraints, the power system for battery constraints. Hence each barrier function B_i need only be synthesised in the low‑dimensional subspace of the relevant objects, rather than in the full latent space. This reduces the HJ reachability problem from \mathcal{O}(d^3) to \mathcal{O}(N_{\text{obj}} \cdot d_{\text{obj}}^3) complexity.

Step 2: Latent Reachability with Uncertainty Inflation. In each relevant subspace, we solve the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman (HJ) equation (Bansal et al., 2017) using the symplectic predictor as the dynamics model. The solution V_i(z) is the signed distance to the unsafe set — positive for safe states, negative for unsafe ones. Following the uncertainty‑aware approach of Seo, Nakamura, and Bajcsy (2025), we inflate the unsafe boundary by a margin \sigma_i(z) = \kappa \sqrt{U(z)}, where U(z) is aggregated over the objects affecting daemon i. The inflated barrier is B_i(z) = V_i(z) - \sigma_i(z).

Step 3: Safety Filter as a Quadratic Program. At each control cycle, the filter solves

\begin{aligned}
a_{\text{safe}} = \arg\min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \quad &\|a - a_{\text{raw}}\|^2 \\
\text{subject to} \quad &\nabla B_i(z)^{\top} f_\theta(z, a) \geq -\alpha_i B_i(z), \quad \forall i \in \{1, \ldots, K\}.
\end{aligned}

This is the standard Control Barrier Function (CBF) quadratic program (Ames, Xu, Grizzle, & Tabuada, 2017). The constraints ensure forward invariance of the safe set: the time derivative of B_i along the trajectory is bounded below, preventing the barrier from crossing zero. If the QP is infeasible — which can only occur at the boundary of the safe set — the filter activates a model‑free emergency brake: a_{\text{safe}} = \mathbf{0}.

Step 4: Probabilistic Guarantee. The conjugacy error \varepsilon_{\text{conj}} from Theorem 4.1 represents the maximum deviation between the true system state and the latent state predicted by the symplectic model. For a given state with uncertainty U(z_t), the probability that the true state lies outside the inflated safe margin \kappa \sqrt{U(z_t)} is bounded, by Chebyshev's inequality, by \varepsilon_{\text{conj}}^2 / (\kappa^2 U(z_t)).

Given M independent Monte Carlo rollouts, the probability that all M miss a true safety violation of probability p is (1 - p)^M \leq e^{-p M}. In the worst case for low‑uncertainty states, U(z_t) \geq \tau, so p \leq \varepsilon_{\text{conj}}^2 / (\kappa^2 \tau). Substituting M_{\text{eff}} for the adaptive sampling budget yields the first term. The polynomial term \mathcal{O}(N(\delta + \Delta t^{p+1})) accounts for the systematic conjugacy error that cannot be reduced by increased sampling — it can only be reduced by improving the encoder–decoder quality (\delta) or the integrator order (p). A union bound over the N-step horizon completes the proof. ∎

7.4 Implementation: Latent Safety Filter

The barrier functions B_i are pre‑trained by solving the HJ equation on a numerical grid of 10^5 points in each object subspace and distilling the solution into a small, five‑layer ReLU network (thirty‑two neurons per layer). At runtime, the filter evaluates all active daemons in parallel; the QP with five constraints and six action variables is solved using a primal–dual interior‑point method (CVXGEN; Mattingley & Boyd, 2012). The total latency is below two hundred microseconds on the specified GPU.

