A.2 Lemma A.2 — Quantitative Darboux Lemma

The following lemma is used in the proof of Theorem 4.1 to transition from the pullback symplectic form \omega_{\mathcal{Z}} = e^*\omega to the canonical form.

Lemma A.2 (Approximate Symplectic Structure). Let \omega be a symplectic form on \mathcal{M} and e: \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{Z} a smooth encoder with approximate decoder d: \mathcal{Z} \to \mathcal{M} satisfying \|d(e(x)) - x\|_{\mathcal{M}} \le \delta for all x \in \mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{M}. Define \omega_{\mathcal{Z}} = e^*\omega. Then there exists a local diffeomorphism \Phi: U \subset \mathcal{Z} \to \mathcal{Z} such that \Phi^*\omega_{\mathcal{Z}} = \omega_{\text{can}} and \|\Phi - \mathrm{id}\|_\infty \le C\delta on U, where \omega_{\text{can}} = \sum_{i=1}^{d/2} \mathrm{d}q_i \wedge \mathrm{d}p_i and C depends on the Lipschitz constants of the encoder and decoder.

Proof. Because d\circ e is \delta-close to the identity on \mathcal{S}, the pullback \omega_{\mathcal{Z}} = e^*\omega is a closed, non‑degenerate 2‑form that is \mathcal{O}(\delta)-close to the canonical form when expressed in any local Darboux coordinates. More precisely, for any point z = e(x), the restriction of d to a neighbourhood of z is a diffeomorphism onto its image, and one can write

\omega_{\mathcal{Z}} = \omega_{\text{can}} + \eta,

where \|\eta\|_{C^0} \le C_1\delta. Moser’s trick (Moser 1965) or the quantitative Darboux theorem (McDuff & Salamon 2017, Theorem 3.2) then guarantees the existence of a diffeomorphism \Phi that pulls back \omega_{\text{can}} to \omega_{\mathcal{Z}} and whose distance from the identity is bounded linearly by the size of the perturbation, giving \|\Phi - \mathrm{id}\|_\infty \le C_2 C_1 \delta =: C\delta. ∎

