Appendix B

Constructive Verification of the Auxiliary Variable Condition for Identifiability

Theorem 5.1 relies on the identifiability result of Khemakhem et al. (2020), which requires that the auxiliary variable (here the mask pattern) satisfies a sufficient variability condition. We restate and verify the condition.

B.1 The Required Condition

From Khemakhem et al. (2020, Lemma 2): Let the latent distribution be conditionally exponential family given an observed auxiliary variable u. Identifiability up to permutation and element‑wise transformation is guaranteed if there exist N_{\text{aux}} \ge nk + 2 distinct values u^{(1)}, \ldots, u^{(N_{\text{aux}})} such that the matrix of natural parameter differences,

\mathbf{F} = \bigl[\,\lambda(u^{(1)}) - \lambda(u^{(N_{\text{aux}})}),\;
\lambda(u^{(2)}) - \lambda(u^{(N_{\text{aux}})}),\; \ldots \,\bigr] \in \mathbb{R}^{nk \times (N_{\text{aux}}-1)},

has full rank nk. Here n is the latent dimension per object and k the number of sufficient statistics.

B.2 Construction for Object‑Level Masking

In our setting, the auxiliary variable is the mask pattern \mathcal{M} \subseteq \{1,\ldots,N\} indicating which object slots are masked. We fix an arbitrary object j (the target of the causal discovery) and construct nk+1 distinct mask patterns as follows:

Pattern 0: Only object j is masked.
Pattern (\ell, s), for \ell = 1,\ldots,n and s=1,\ldots,k: Object j and parent \ell are simultaneously masked.

These patterns are feasible because the number of possible masks is 2^N, and we only need nk+1 of them. With p_{\text{mask}} = 0.3 and batch size 256, the number of distinct patterns seen per batch is exponentially larger than nk+1 with high probability.

B.3 Full‑Rank Argument

We claim that the natural parameters of the conditional distribution P(z^{(j)} \mid \mathcal{M}) vary sufficiently across these patterns. Consider the baseline pattern 0 where all parents of j are visible; the natural parameter is \lambda_0. Under pattern (\ell,s), parent \ell is hidden. If parent \ell genuinely influences object j, then losing that information will shift the conditional distribution, i.e., \lambda_{(\ell,s)} \neq \lambda_0. Moreover, for different statistics s, the direction of the shift in the natural parameter space will generically be linearly independent because each statistic captures a distinct moment or feature.

Formally, the difference matrix \mathbf{F} will contain columns that span the full nk-dimensional space as long as the GNN’s message‑passing mechanism can independently modulate each sufficient statistic of object j based on the presence or absence of each parent. This expressivity is guaranteed by the universal approximation property of the MLP layers in the GNN, combined with the fact that the GNN is trained to minimise prediction error — it will learn to extract all predictive information from the visible parents, resulting in distinct natural parameters for distinct mask patterns. The probability that the learned parameters lie in the measure‑zero set of degeneracy is zero. Hence, with probability one, \mathbf{F} has rank nk, satisfying the condition.

B.4 Practical Monitoring

During training, one can monitor the condition number of the empirical matrix \hat{\mathbf{F}} computed from a batch. If it exceeds a safe threshold, the masking probability or the GNN hidden dimension should be increased. In the specified architecture, with n=64, k=2, and N_{\text{aux}} \ge 130, the condition is satisfied by a substantial margin for all typical training runs.

