Appendix D

Derivation of the Safe Quadratic Program from Inflated HJ Reachability

This appendix derives the safety filter and the probabilistic guarantee of Theorem 7.1.

D.1 Hamilton–Jacobi Reachability in Latent Subspaces

For each COH constraint daemon D_i, we identify the subset of objects \mathcal{O}_i whose states influence the constraint. By Theorem 5.1, these subsets are causally independent, so we can treat each daemon separately in its low‑dimensional latent subspace.

In the subspace of daemon i, we train a safety value function V_i(z) that solves the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation:

\min\!\bigl\{ h_i(z),\; V_i(z) \bigr\} = 0,

with the boundary condition that V_i(z) = h_i(z) on the constraint surface h_i(z) = 0. This is solved numerically on a grid of approximately 10^5 points using the symplectic predictor as the dynamics model, yielding a signed distance to the unsafe set. The computation is performed offline during the safety‑filter pretraining phase (Chapter 8, Phase 5).

D.2 Uncertainty‑Aware Barrier Inflation

The conjugacy error \varepsilon_{\text{conj}} = C_1\delta + C_2(\Delta t)^{p+1} (Theorem 4.1) measures the worst‑case deviation between the latent prediction and the true system state. To maintain safety despite this error, we inflate the unsafe boundary by a margin proportional to the epistemic uncertainty U(z_t) from the Var‑JEPA module:

B_i(z) = V_i(z) - \kappa \sqrt{U(z)},

where \kappa > 0 is a scaling factor chosen such that the inflated safe set remains non‑empty for all typical operational states.

D.3 Control Barrier Function Quadratic Program

The function B_i is a valid Control Barrier Function (CBF) (Ames et al., 2017) if there exists a class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha_i such that for all safe z,

\dot{B}_i(z) = \nabla B_i(z)^{\top} f_\theta(z,a) \ge -\alpha_i B_i(z).

Forward invariance of the set \{z \mid B_i(z) \ge 0\} then guarantees that the system never enters the inflated unsafe set.

At each control cycle, the planner proposes a raw action a_{\text{raw}} based on the current latent state and the planning objective. The safety filter solves the quadratic program:

\begin{aligned}
a_{\text{safe}} = \arg\min_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \quad & \|a - a_{\text{raw}}\|^2 \\
\text{subject to} \quad & \nabla B_i(z)^{\top} f_\theta(z,a) \ge -\alpha_i B_i(z), \qquad \forall i \in \{1,\ldots,K\}.
\end{aligned}

This QP is convex (quadratic objective, linear constraints) and can be solved in microseconds using a primal–dual interior‑point method (CVXGEN, Mattingley & Boyd 2012).

Feasibility. If the current state is strictly inside the safe set (B_i(z) > 0 for all i), then the zero action a = \mathbf{0} (rover stops) always satisfies the CBF constraints because \nabla B_i^{\top} f_\theta(z,\mathbf{0}) is bounded below by a constant times B_i(z) (by continuity of the dynamics). Thus the QP is feasible, and the closest action to the planner’s proposal is selected. If the state is at the boundary (B_i(z) \le 0 for some i), the filter discards the planner’s action entirely and engages the emergency brake: a_{\text{safe}} = \mathbf{0}.

D.4 Probabilistic Safety Guarantee

For a given state z_t, the conjugacy error \|z_t^{\text{true}} - z_t^{\text{latent}}\| is at most \varepsilon_{\text{conj}}. The probability that the true state lies outside the inflated safe margin is bounded by Chebyshev’s inequality:

P(\text{safety violation at } z_t) \le \frac{\mathbb{E}\bigl[\|z_t^{\text{true}} - z_t^{\text{latent}}\|^2\bigr]}{\kappa^2 U(z_t)} \le \frac{\varepsilon_{\text{conj}}^2}{\kappa^2 U(z_t)}.

Chebyshev’s inequality applies because the variance of the latent state prediction error is bounded by the epistemic uncertainty U(z_t) (by Pinsker’s inequality and the Gaussian structure).

During planning, the safety of an action is checked by simulating M rollouts. In the worst case for low‑uncertainty states, U(z_t) \ge \tau, so the per‑rollout violation probability is at most p = \varepsilon_{\text{conj}}^2/(\kappa^2 \tau). The probability that all M independent rollouts miss a true violation is (1-p)^M \le e^{-p M}. Substituting the effective sample budget M_{\text{eff}} = \rho M_{\text{base}} + (1-\rho) M_{\text{high}} and taking a union bound over the N steps yields:

P(\text{unsafe}) \le N \exp\!\left(-\frac{M_{\text{eff}} \, \varepsilon_{\text{conj}}^2}{\kappa^2 \tau}\right).

Adding the deterministic systematic error \mathcal{O}(N\varepsilon_{\text{conj}}) that cannot be eliminated by sampling completes the bound of Theorem 7.1.

 